// scale.ron — the ONE truth about sizes. Given by the user, 2026-08-09.
//
// All lengths in METERS (1 Bevy unit = 1 meter, docs/conventions.md).
//
// ⚠️ These numbers are **NOT untuned** — and that is the difference from game.ron:7,
// maps.ron:15 and titan.ron:7. There stand starting values that anyone may change. **Here
// stand given values.** Whoever changes them changes no assumption but a DECISION OF THE
// USER — and has to ask him, not measure.
//
// The precedence rule that goes with it: **a direct figure in meters from the user beats
// any derivation.** Wherever the backlog conversion was used so far (0.28 m/stud,
// docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-002) and the result contradicts this file, this file holds. The
// conversion stays for everything the user has said nothing about.
//
// This file is the SOURCE, game.ron / maps.ron / titan.ron are the CONSUMERS: they fetch
// their sizes from here, and tests/data.rs falls over the moment one of the three drifts
// away from it. A size stands exactly once — otherwise two truths drift apart, and nobody
// notices until a titan sticks out through a roof.
(
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The three scales — the world is DELIBERATELY not scaled uniformly.
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // Architecture stands at 1.0: a house is as big as a house is. Titans are exaggerated
    // by 1.4, walls by 2.4. A titan thus looks bigger than he would "realistically" be, a
    // wall monumental — and that is exactly the visual language of the reference work: the
    // human is small, the threat out of all proportion, the wall a horizon.
    //
    // **That is a design decision, not an inaccuracy.** Whoever "corrects" the three
    // factors later because some number strikes him as unrealistic destroys the visual
    // language and notices it only when the game looks arbitrary. The factors stand here
    // as numbers so that you SEE that they differ — not so that you level them out.
    // (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
    architecture_factor: 1.0,
    titan_factor: 1.4,
    wall_factor: 2.4,

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Reference — what the eye measures everything else against
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    reference: (
        // "Check the capsule exactly!" — the user, explicitly. The player capsule in
        // game.ron:player.height_m MUST be this number, not roughly. Every size comparison
        // in the game hangs on it. (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
        human_height_m: 1.8,
        // A door is the second scale that every viewer reads without noticing.
        door_height_m: 2.1,
        // "Keep them narrow" — the user. Narrow streets are no detail, they are the reason
        // why speed in the Vector Gear feels like speed. maps.ron:layout.street_m lies in
        // this window, tests/data.rs checks it. (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
        min_street_m: 6.0,
        max_street_m: 8.0,
        // A human head is about 1/7.5 of the body height. The value stands here because it
        // is the COMPARISON: the titan head is relatively SMALLER (see titan.*_head_fraction),
        // and that is how the eye reads "the thing is huge" instead of "the thing is near".
        human_head_fraction: 0.1333,
    ),

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Architecture (factor 1.0)
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // ⚠️ The residential stock is FLAT: 4.5 to 11.5 m. That is intended.
    // The vertical of this game comes from the wall (120 m), the church (35 m), the
    // watchtower and the trees — **not** from the residential houses. A city of 30 m blocks
    // would be no reference look but a skyline, and the wall would lose its effect.
    //
    // ⚠️ And that is exactly why church, watchtower and tree are NO decoration here: they
    // are the only anchors above 11.5 m. From an 11.5 m roof and a 3.0 m minimum rope
    // follows an anchor ceiling of 14.5 m — above that no rope holds on a residential
    // house. The cortex of `large` sits at 12.5 m, of `huge` at 18.7 m, of `boss` at 24.9 m.
    // A city without landmarks therefore leaves three of five classes attackable only
    // ballistically (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-022). tests/data.rs pins down that for every class
    // a structure above its cortex height exists — and that the current map has really
    // placed at least one of them.
    architecture: (
        heights_m: {
            "house_small":  4.5,   // 1 story
            "house_town":  8.0,   // 2 stories
            "house_large": 11.5,   // 3 stories — the upper limit of normal building
            // 18.0 — the class that did not exist, and Q-036 is the question that named the
            // hole: between the 11.5 m house and the 35 m church there was nothing at all, so
            // a district built out of the residential band alone has 5 m of spread over 700 m
            // of town and reads flat from any distance. The user, 2026-08-13: *"lass es wie
            // die echte stadt aussehen! aktuell kann man es noch nicht erkennen!"*.
            // 18 m is 1.6x the large house — a merchant's house, a granary, a guild hall: the
            // building a real walled town has a handful of and never a street full of, which
            // is why maps.ron spends it through `layout.tall_fraction` and not through the
            // band. It stays well under the church (35 m) that carries the skyline.
            "house_tall":  18.0,   // 5 stories — rare, and the reason the roofscape has relief
            "tree":       12.0,   // foreground layering: gives depth a ladder
            "watchtower":   12.0,   // standing on the wall
            "church":     35.0,   // bell tower. LANDMARK, not a grid house
        },
        // Eaves = top edge of the outer wall, where the roof starts. The difference to the
        // total height is the roof — and the roof is the surface you land on.
        // Entered only where the user has named a number; whoever needs another one asks
        // him instead of inventing it. (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
        eaves_m: {
            "house_small": 3.0,
            "house_town": 6.0,
        },
    ),

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Titans (factor 1.4)
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    titan: (
        // **The cortex sits at about 89 % of the body height.** That is the only lethal
        // weak point (F-030) — and until 2026-08-09 this number stood NOWHERE.
        //
        // ⚠️ This fraction is a **CHECK RULE, not a source.** Until 2026-08-09 the five
        // cortex heights were computed from it — that broke our own precedence rule: the
        // user names five **figures in meters** (3.7 · 8.9 · 12.5 · 18.7 · 24.9), and a
        // direct figure in meters beats any derivation. Computed from 0.89, the small class
        // came out 4 cm off (4.2 × 0.89 = 3.738 instead of 3.7 — +1.03 %).
        // Now the user's five numbers stand in `classes`, and this fraction is what the
        // user wrote next to them (his words: "~89 %"): the guard that notices when one of
        // the five drifts away (tests/data.rs holds 0.88..0.90).
        //
        // It is still used as a SOURCE only where the user has named no cortex height —
        // today exactly one case: the Ashwalker (150 m ⇒ 133.5 m).
        // (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
        cortex_fraction: 0.89,

        // The titan head is 1/9 to 1/10 of the height — RELATIVELY SMALLER than on a human
        // (1/7.5, see reference.human_head_fraction). That is the trick that makes a model
        // look big: too large a head makes every titan look like a doll, no matter how many
        // meters the data sheet says. (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
        //
        // The upper bound is ROUNDED UP: 1/9 = 0.111111…, and with 0.1111 a model that
        // takes the user exactly at his word falls just outside our own window. 0.1112 is
        // the smallest number that still includes 1/9.
        min_head_fraction: 0.1,      // 1/10
        max_head_fraction: 0.1112,   // 1/9 = 0.111111…, rounded up

        // The five size classes (F-064). titan.ron names ONLY the class per kind; height
        // and cortex height come from here — otherwise there are sixteen numbers nobody
        // checks against each other.
        //
        // **Both columns are figures in meters from the user**, neither is computed. That
        // is the more expensive but correct way: the guard above checks their ratio instead
        // of producing it — that makes it stronger, not weaker. (2026-08-09.)
        classes: {
            "small":        (height_m:  4.2, cortex_height_m:  3.7),
            "medium":       (height_m: 10.0, cortex_height_m:  8.9),
            "large": (height_m: 14.0, cortex_height_m: 12.5),
            "huge":        (height_m: 21.0, cortex_height_m: 18.7),
            // The user names the class "Abnormal / Boss". Whether he means the size class
            // by that or the titan kind that the project vocabulary calls "Errant" is
            // open — docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-020. It is deliberately still used by NO kind in
            // titan.ron: the first representative is a raid opponent, and raids are
            // behind the Vector Gear gate (docs/ROADMAP.md).
            "boss":         (height_m: 28.0, cortex_height_m: 24.9),
        },

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------
        // The box rig (F-050, F-056). ⚠️ UNTUNED — invented by the main head on
        // 2026-08-09 against no measurement, unlike everything above it, which the
        // user laid down. Whoever changes one of these six changes a guess, not a
        // decision. They are fractions of the body height so that one number per
        // class stays the only source of scale.
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------

        // **The biggest single hole in the repository until today: nothing said how
        // wide a titan is.** No collider, no rig, and no answer to the alley
        // question could be given without it. 0.25 makes a `large` titan 3.5 m
        // wide in a 7.0 m street (maps.ron: layout.street_m) — 1.75 m clearance per
        // side. At `boss` (28 m) it is exactly 7.0 m and the alley is blocked by a
        // silent wall; that is why `max_spawnable_class` stands below.
        width_fraction: 0.25,

        // The four parts of the rig, stacked. They are chosen to ADD UP against the
        // numbers that are already laid down, and that is the whole reason they are
        // not round: legs 0.48 + torso 0.41 = 0.89 = `cortex_fraction`, so the
        // cortex sits exactly on the seam between torso and head, which is what a
        // nape is. The head then occupies 0.89..1.00 = 0.11, inside the user's
        // window of min_head_fraction..max_head_fraction (0.1 .. 0.1112).
        // A test in tests/data.rs holds that arithmetic — if somebody moves one of
        // them, the cortex ends up in the belly and nobody notices in a screenshot.
        leg_fraction: 0.48,
        torso_fraction: 0.41,
        // Shoulders sit BELOW the cortex, not level with it: an arm that hinges at
        // the nape cannot swing in front of the body. 0.82 of 10 m = 8.2 m.
        shoulder_height_fraction: 0.82,
        // Arm length from the shoulder. 0.44 of a 10 m husk = 4.4 m, so the hand
        // hangs at 3.8 m and the wind-up carries it about 8.8 m — that travel is
        // what F-053's telegraph has to be visible from 40 m.
        arm_fraction: 0.44,

        // The three pose angles of the wind-up and the strike (F-053). ⚠️ UNTUNED.
        // They stand HERE and not per kind in titan.ron on purpose: they are
        // properties of the rig, and the rig is one rig. Per kind they would be
        // twenty-seven invented numbers instead of three, and nobody would ever
        // tune twenty-seven. A kind that needs its own telegraph later gets an
        // override then — that is a smaller change than un-inventing 27 numbers.
        //
        // "Raise the arm 140 degrees" must never live in Rust (rule 2): an angle in
        // code is an angle nobody tunes after the first person plays it.
        windup_arm_deg: 140.0,
        windup_lean_deg: 12.0,
        // Negative: the strike swings the arm back DOWN past the rest pose.
        strike_arm_deg: -30.0,

        // **The class cap for this session, and it is a user decision I made in his
        // absence** — docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-028, with the assumption and the place to
        // take it back. A `huge` titan is 5.25 m wide in a 7.0 m street and a `boss`
        // is 7.00 m in a 7.00 m street. The second is not tight, it is a wall.
        // Taking it back is this one line.
        max_spawnable_class: "large",

        // The 150 m boss. Stands DELIBERATELY outside `classes`: he is no enemy type you
        // scale, but a structure with a face. The user's check: 150 − 120 = **30 m above
        // the wall**. tests/data.rs pins the 30 down.
        // Name after docs/conventions.md §2. (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
        ashwalker_height_m: 150.0,
    ),

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Walls (factor 2.4)
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    wall: (
        height_m: 120.0,
        // Narrower at the top than at the bottom: the wall is battered. The difference
        // (45 − 28 = 17 m over 120 m of height) is the slope a hook holds on.
        top_thickness_m: 28.0,
        base_thickness_m: 45.0,
        // Stopover at half height: with 90 m of anchor range the wall crown is NOT
        // reachable from below in one move (120 > 90). The platform at 60 m turns an
        // impossible wall into two possible moves. tests/data.rs checks that both legs
        // stay within range. (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
        //
        // Recomputed with the slope ((45 − 28)/2 = 8.5 m of inset over 120 m, 4.05 degrees):
        // ground -> platform 60.15 m ✓ · platform -> crown 60.15 m ✓ · ground -> crown
        // directly 120.30 m ✗. The arithmetic confirms the number — but it is NOT the
        // reason for the platform: the highest point of the flank that lies within 90 m of
        // the wall foot is y = 89.78 m, so the crown would be reachable in two free moves
        // (90 + 30) as well. The platform only carries weight once the FLANK is not
        // anchorable and only platform and crown are anchors — and that is decided nowhere:
        // docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-023.
        platform_height_m: 60.0,

        // ---- The scale ladder -------------------------------------------------
        // A 120 m wall without structure is a gray surface: the eye has nothing to read
        // size off, and the wall looks like a 12 m wall seen from close up. The visible
        // joints of a 0.6 m stone course and a band every 15 m are NO decoration — they
        // are the reason why the wall looks big.
        // (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
        stone_course_m: 0.6,
        band_spacing_m: 15.0,
    ),

    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Camera and Vector Gear
    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    camera: (
        // Eye height. The user names 1.60 m — that beats any derivation from the body
        // height. game.ron:player.eye_height_m MUST be this number.
        // (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
        height_m: 1.6,
        // "FOV ground combat 55-65 degrees — biggest lever". The user says GROUND COMBAT
        // explicitly: that is the base, not the upper limit. When flying fast the field of
        // view widens (F-017 „Geschwindigkeits-Feedback"), and the upper value for that
        // stands in game.ron:camera.fov_max_speed_deg. game.ron:camera.fov_deg has to fall
        // into this window, tests/data.rs checks it. (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
        //
        // ⚠️ **Read VERTICALLY** — and nobody decided that, it follows from Bevy:
        // src/render/mod.rs puts the value into `PerspectiveProjection.fov`, and Bevy
        // documents the field as "The vertical field of view (FOV) in radians"
        // (bevy_camera-0.19.0/src/projection.rs:284-287). On 16:9, 60 degrees vertical
        // = 91.5 degrees horizontal. Were the user's figure meant HORIZONTALLY — the
        // usual reading of an "FOV" number — 32.6..39.4 would have to stand here. The
        // factor between the two readings is 1.7, and the user calls exactly this value
        // his "biggest lever": docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-021.
        min_ground_fov_deg: 55.0,
        max_ground_fov_deg: 65.0,
    ),

    vector: (
        // **90 m, straight from the user** — not 112 m out of the backlog conversion. The
        // precedence rule: a direct figure in meters beats any derivation
        // (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-002). game.ron:vector.hook_range_m MUST be this number.
        // (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
        //
        // **90 -> 200 on 2026-08-10, and the user overrides his own earlier figure**: after
        // playing it he said *"zudem muss die hook range sehr viel laenger sein!"*. The same
        // precedence rule applies as before — a direct instruction beats the number it
        // replaces, including his own (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-035).
        //
        // Why 200 and not 150 or 400: the graybox is 400 m across, so 200 m is the largest
        // range that still makes WHERE you stand a decision rather than a formality — at 400
        // every anchor in the world is always in reach and the map stops existing. 200 m also
        // reaches the church (35 m) from more than half the city, which is what FIND-026 says
        // is missing. ⚠️ UNTUNED, and it is the user's number to move.
        //
        // **200 -> 500 on 2026-08-12, and he moved it himself**: *"und das seil muss deutlich
        // deutlich schneller gespannt werden. nicht frame perfekt aber mit ca 500m pro sekunde.
        // mit der range 500 meter!"* (docs/NEXT.md §1A). Third time the same precedence rule
        // decides it and third time in the same direction — a direct figure in metres from the
        // user beats every derivation, including the two figures he gave before.
        //
        // What the 200 m argument above cost, stated so nobody re-derives it: it was written
        // against a **400 m graybox**, and that map no longer ships. Ashgate is 700 m across,
        // so 500 m is 71 % of the district's edge, not 125 % of it — "where you stand" is
        // still a decision, but a much looser one, and that IS what he asked for. The map is
        // the lever that keeps it honest from here on: a district under ~1000 m across cannot
        // make a 500 m rope a positional choice again.
        // ⚠️ UNTUNED, and still the user's number to move.
        anchor_range_m: 500.0,
        // "Vector Gear speed x1.5 vs. standard" — the factor is given, but the REFERENCE
        // is missing: x1.5 of what? Running (6 m/s) would be 9 m/s and thus absurdly slow
        // for a Vector Gear; the speed limit (75 m/s) would be 112.5 m/s. The number stands
        // here so that it does not get lost — it is NOT computed with until the user
        // answers (docs/QUESTIONS.md Q-018). (Given by the user, 2026-08-09.)
        speed_factor: 1.5,
    ),
)
